class Student {
    int marks;

    Student(int marks) {
        this.marks = marks;
    }
}

public class Main {

    static void change(int x, Student s) {
        x = 100;
        s.marks = 95;
        s = new Student(50);
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {

        int n = 20;
        Student st = new Student(60);

        change(n, st);

        System.out.println(n);
        System.out.println(st.marks);
    }
}
1.Why does n retain its original value?
Because Java passes primitive values by value. Changing x does not change the original variable n.
2. Why does st.marks change?
Because s refers to the same Student object initially. Changing s.marks = 95 changes the object's marks.
3. Why does assigning new Student(50) to s not change st?
Because s is a copy of the reference. Assigning a new object to s does not change the original reference st.
4. Is Java pass-by-value or pass-by-reference?
Java always uses Pass-by-Value. Even object references are passed by value.
